# Theorem 6: `I_k` has exactly three minimal generators

The bounded trial recommended by `lean-formalization-plan.org`: the smallest
dependency footprint in the article, chosen to give a real signal on
formalization pace for this project's style of argument.

The article proves Theorem 6 from two lemmas:

* **Lemma (generic).** A homogeneous ideal generated by finitely many forms of
  the same degree, linearly independent over the base field, has that generating
  set as a *minimal* one.
* **Lemma (independence).** The three components of `w_k` are linearly
  independent over `F`, for every `k ≥ 2`.

This file does the first. It is the general algebra, it is what the article's own
proof leans on, and it is stateable without any of the commutator construction —
so it isolates "how hard is mathlib to work with here" from "how hard is this
particular ideal".

The article's proof, in one sentence: *a polynomial-coefficient combination
reproducing one generator from the others, while staying in the same degree, can
only use degree-0 coefficients, so a redundant generator would contradict linear
independence directly.*  The formal version below is that argument with the
degree bookkeeping made explicit.
-/
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPolynomial.Homogeneous
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Defs
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Span

open MvPolynomial

variable {σ : Type*} {F : Type*} [Field F]

namespace KCommuting

/-- **Minimality of independent same-degree generators.**

If `g : Fin n → MvPolynomial σ F` are all homogeneous of one common degree `d`
and linearly independent over `F`, then no `g i` lies in the ideal generated by
the others — i.e. the generating set is minimal.

This is the article's "independent same-degree generators are minimal", stated
for a polynomial ring rather than an arbitrary graded ring.  The restriction is
deliberate and costs nothing here: the article only ever applies it to
`R_k = ℚ[v_1,…,v_k]`, and `MvPolynomial`'s `IsHomogeneous` /
`homogeneousComponent` API is far better developed in mathlib than the general
`GradedAlgebra` one. -/
theorem not_mem_span_others_of_linearIndependent
    {n : ℕ} (d : ℕ) (g : Fin n → MvPolynomial σ F)
    (hhom : ∀ i, (g i).IsHomogeneous d)
    (hind : LinearIndependent F g) (i : Fin n) :
    g i ∉ Ideal.span (g '' {j | j ≠ i}) := by
  sorry

/-- The specialisation actually used for Theorem 6: three generators. -/
theorem three_generators_minimal
    (d : ℕ) (g : Fin 3 → MvPolynomial σ F)
    (hhom : ∀ i, (g i).IsHomogeneous d)
    (hind : LinearIndependent F g) (i : Fin 3) :
    g i ∉ Ideal.span (g '' {j | j ≠ i}) :=
  not_mem_span_others_of_linearIndependent d g hhom hind i

end KCommuting
